1
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:17,280
At first, this place seems almost empty.
Salt. Mountains. Water. Then something moves.

2
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:25,522
Welcome to Laguna Chaxa, in Chile’s
Atacama Desert. We’re going to stay awhile.

3
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:34,140
What could a flamingo find
to eat in a place like this?

4
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:55,463
Look just ahead, a little to your right.
Watch what happens to that curved bill.

5
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:04,830
The flamingo lowers its head and
turns its bill upside down in the water.

6
00:01:20,650 --> 00:01:26,107
Its bill works like a filter. Water passes
through; tiny pieces of food stay behind.

7
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:33,621
We may not see the meal. We
can see the bird finding it.

8
00:01:40,650 --> 00:01:45,556
Now watch one foot. A small step sends
circles across the whole reflection.

9
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:04,830
Farther out, another bird is resting
on one leg. See if you can find it.

10
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:15,008
Some feed. Some rest. Take a moment
to choose which one you want to watch.

11
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:33,177
Listen now. The lagoon
has more than one voice.

12
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:55,575
Flamingos live in groups. Across the shallows,
their calls become part of the landscape.

13
00:03:00,650 --> 00:03:05,023
Try following one bird for a few steps.
Let the others carry on around it.

14
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:24,280
There’s another clue in those
feathers. Where does the pink come from?

15
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:35,305
Color pigments in a flamingo’s food
help give its feathers their pink shades.

16
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:44,893
Food becomes part of the bird. Even
its color carries a little of its world.

17
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:04,655
The water looked empty when we
arrived. Does it look empty to you now?

18
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:14,245
We found a meal, a resting
place, and a gathering of birds.

19
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:24,972
One lagoon. Many small lives. And
plenty we still haven’t noticed.

20
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:45,395
Where shall we go next: beneath a coral
sea, or beside an African waterhole?
